\ul How to Play\ulnone\par
\ul Start of game\ulnone : put out the board; shuffle the Rooms deck and deal one out (face-down) to each Room space.  Then put red Denizen and yellow Loot chits on board spaces as indicated and the Cats on their indicated starting spaces.  If Dragon is playing, he goes first, otherwise whomever has most recently pet a cat goes first.  From first player to the left, each player chooses a character and puts their matching colored pawn on the Main Entrance space, draws the character's specified number of starting health tokens.  Shuffle Room, Loot and Guardian decks separately.  Players take turns in order from first player going left.\par
\ul\par
During the game\ulnone : \par
\tab -at any point once the first player's turn starts, if a number is ever rolled twice in a row for any (game)reason, the player who first calls out "double!" can immediately move cat C 1d6 in the direction of their choice.  If they roll that same number a third time, another player could immediately call "double" on that.\par
\tab -whenever a cat is being moved, if it runs into or stops on a player's token, the Cat stops and player loses their choice of a Loot card to the Treasure Pile or a health token if they have no Loot\par
\tab -when a player loses all health, they must discard all Loot, then miss their next turn to catch their breath, then start at half their starting health\par
\tab -scrolls that affect a roll have to be played before any die rolls happen\par
\tab -if a player's ENC total (from Loot) exceeds their strength, they must discard Loot until it doesn't.\ul\par
\par
Player's turn\ulnone : \par
\tab 1. player rolls the d6, can move own their token that many spaces in the direction of the arrows.  If they enter a room, they must stop.  If they rolled a 1 or a 6, they can then move cat A or B 1d6 spaces in the direction of their choice.  If they run into a cat, they must stop and lose their choice of a Loot card to the Treasure Pile or a health token if they have no Loot.  \par
\tab 2. if they stopped in a room with any chits on it, resolve them: \par
\tab\tab a. If there are any red chits on the space, the player draws a Denizen card; it will have either a Might or Guile score (or both and say which to use); the player must use the corresponding attribute to calculate their combat score = (base might OR guile) + (any room or scroll bonus); the Denizen's is shown on the card.  The greater score wins, Denizens win ties.  If the player loses, they lose one health token, the Denizen card is discarded and their turn ends.  If the player wins, discard the red chit and Denizen card, repeat until red chits are gone, then and goto (b).\par
\tab\tab b. If there are any yellow chits on the space, discard all yellow chits on the space and draw a Loot card per chit.\par
\ul\par
End of Game\ulnone : the final Room is the Dragon's Den.  The Dragon is a Denizen with both Might and Guile of something high.  If the player bests them, they escape the Den, if not they lose a health, and if they lose all health at the Dragon, they are out of the game (=die).  The first player to best the Dragon gets 2 Loot draws, the second gets 1, others get zero.  Once all players have escaped or died, the escaped player with the highest Treasure value wins.\par

